1
00:00:12,711 --> 00:00:16,921
(The last chance to advance to Round 3)

2
00:00:17,121 --> 00:00:19,620
(Cheorwon, the regenerative clean city)

3
00:00:19,621 --> 00:00:21,490
(And 8 teams have gathered here.)

4
00:00:21,491 --> 00:00:22,750
(Michelin couple, Kim Hee Eun, 2nd place in Round 1, Lim Ki Hak)

5
00:00:22,751 --> 00:00:24,120
(Busan's best, Kwak Dong Hoon, Brand predator, Kim Ho Yoon)

6
00:00:24,121 --> 00:00:25,560
(Seochon crown prince, Lee Jae Hoon, Franchise master, Kim Kwan Hoon)

7
00:00:25,561 --> 00:00:26,930
(Jo Seo Hyung and Shin Ga Young)

8
00:00:26,931 --> 00:00:28,491
The theme of the revival match is...

9
00:00:28,861 --> 00:00:30,460
(A great restaurant always comes back with rice.)

10
00:00:30,461 --> 00:00:32,361
(Cheorwon Odae Rice)

11
00:00:32,431 --> 00:00:34,770
(Grown with the clean water of Hantan River...)

12
00:00:34,771 --> 00:00:37,270
(and in vast plains that are rich with minerals)

13
00:00:37,271 --> 00:00:40,710
(Create a new dish that showcases the value of Odae Rice)

14
00:00:40,711 --> 00:00:43,071
(Today's reserved guests)

15
00:00:43,481 --> 00:00:46,950
(Those who have enjoyed Odae Rice in Cheorwon for many years)

16
00:00:46,951 --> 00:00:49,180
(100 elderly women)

17
00:00:49,181 --> 00:00:50,981
How should we approach this mission?

18
00:00:51,651 --> 00:00:52,881
This is cooking for mom.

19
00:00:54,251 --> 00:00:55,351
What does my mom like?

20
00:00:55,951 --> 00:00:57,261
Serve 100 guests...

21
00:00:57,691 --> 00:00:59,560
a dish that uses rice...

22
00:00:59,561 --> 00:01:02,331
or goes well with rice.

23
00:01:02,991 --> 00:01:05,761
Each team must prepare one plate...

24
00:01:05,931 --> 00:01:09,331
to fill the octagonal dish that will be used for the revival mission.

25
00:01:09,501 --> 00:01:10,771
It's Chinese-style cuisine.

26
00:01:12,101 --> 00:01:13,911
The plates fall into place perfectly.

27
00:01:14,141 --> 00:01:17,941
Each guest will receive 1 10-dollar bill and 10 1-dollar bills.

28
00:01:18,181 --> 00:01:20,181
For a total of 20 dollars,

29
00:01:20,281 --> 00:01:23,051
they'll decide the price of your dish and pay for it.

30
00:01:23,551 --> 00:01:25,157
The guests determine the price of our dish?

31
00:01:25,181 --> 00:01:27,790
Only the two teams with the highest sales...

32
00:01:27,791 --> 00:01:30,961
will advance to the third round without closing their business.

33
00:01:31,191 --> 00:01:32,490
I'm always confident.

34
00:01:32,491 --> 00:01:33,930
I'm feeling lucky today.

35
00:01:33,931 --> 00:01:35,790
Something elderly women would love.

36
00:01:35,791 --> 00:01:38,200
Let's make something familiar yet new.

37
00:01:38,201 --> 00:01:40,000
What's clear is that we'll compete with flavour.

38
00:01:40,001 --> 00:01:42,641
Let's give it everything we've got like it's our last performance.

39
00:01:43,371 --> 00:01:46,540
Each team will receive 200 dollars for ingredients.

40
00:01:46,541 --> 00:01:48,810
- Two hundred dollars?
- You can go to the Grocery Store...

41
00:01:48,811 --> 00:01:50,240
to buy supplies.

42
00:01:50,241 --> 00:01:52,522
- It's ten dollars per person.
- The budget is too small.

43
00:01:52,611 --> 00:01:56,111
The reservation time is 12:30pm.

44
00:01:56,251 --> 00:01:57,251
(5 hours from now)

45
00:01:57,252 --> 00:01:59,197
- We don't have much time.
- There's not a lot of time.

46
00:01:59,221 --> 00:02:01,850
The Arena Grocery Store provides basic seasoning,

47
00:02:01,851 --> 00:02:04,221
equipment, and kitchen tools.

48
00:02:04,361 --> 00:02:06,830
However, the quantity of equipment...

49
00:02:06,831 --> 00:02:08,631
and tools is limited.

50
00:02:08,791 --> 00:02:10,230
It's all about the equipment.

51
00:02:10,231 --> 00:02:11,760
Let's make sure to take the pressure cooker.

52
00:02:11,761 --> 00:02:13,801
- Here's the Arena Grocery Store.
- Please start...

53
00:02:13,931 --> 00:02:16,171
- your strategy meeting now.
- Last time, it was here.

54
00:02:16,871 --> 00:02:18,631
- Shall we write it down?
- Let's get going.

55
00:02:19,771 --> 00:02:21,641
- What?
- We have to go now.

56
00:02:22,971 --> 00:02:26,581
(Jo Seo Hyung is the first one to run over.)

57
00:02:26,611 --> 00:02:28,611
- What is it? Perilla seeds?
- Perilla seeds.

58
00:02:29,211 --> 00:02:30,451
Green plum syrup.

59
00:02:31,051 --> 00:02:32,571
Let's get two packs of glutinous rice.

60
00:02:33,291 --> 00:02:34,621
And one pancake flour.

61
00:02:39,561 --> 00:02:42,190
(Finishing with a bag of rice)

62
00:02:42,191 --> 00:02:43,300
(The Star In My Heart)

63
00:02:43,301 --> 00:02:45,561
(The free shopping is complete.)

64
00:02:45,761 --> 00:02:47,630
As soon as I saw rice,

65
00:02:47,631 --> 00:02:50,441
the first thing I thought was making side dishes.

66
00:02:51,071 --> 00:02:53,771
I tasted the rice as soon as we arrived.

67
00:02:56,071 --> 00:02:57,111
It's good.

68
00:02:57,741 --> 00:03:00,550
The more you chew Odae Rice, the sweeter it gets.

69
00:03:00,551 --> 00:03:02,850
The moist texture created...

70
00:03:02,851 --> 00:03:04,780
by the natural sweetness...

71
00:03:04,781 --> 00:03:06,580
allows the side dishes to draw you in...

72
00:03:06,581 --> 00:03:08,191
and absorb the flavours.

73
00:03:08,691 --> 00:03:10,620
I thought several different side dishes would...

74
00:03:10,621 --> 00:03:13,291
let people experience a wider range of tastes.

75
00:03:13,761 --> 00:03:16,530
Something with perilla seeds like a mustard-based cold salad.

76
00:03:16,531 --> 00:03:18,531
- It'd go well with scallions.
- A cold salad?

77
00:03:18,601 --> 00:03:20,130
Or we could blanch squid...

78
00:03:20,131 --> 00:03:22,000
and mix it with chestnuts.

79
00:03:22,001 --> 00:03:25,301
Let's toss bracken with squid and perilla oil.

80
00:03:25,641 --> 00:03:27,641
I'm good at making side dishes,

81
00:03:27,941 --> 00:03:29,810
so I will definitely win.

82
00:03:29,811 --> 00:03:30,957
(What side dishes will the Korean cuisine specialist create?)

83
00:03:30,981 --> 00:03:32,741
(Become rich, Number One)

84
00:03:33,211 --> 00:03:34,250
Think about it carefully.

85
00:03:34,251 --> 00:03:35,480
(Number One)

86
00:03:35,481 --> 00:03:36,551
Is it the right choice?

87
00:03:37,251 --> 00:03:39,051
- The total amount per person is...
- Twenty.

88
00:03:39,251 --> 00:03:40,451
It's 20 dollars.

89
00:03:40,821 --> 00:03:41,861
That's the maximum amount.

90
00:03:43,121 --> 00:03:45,190
What will make us earn 20 dollars?

91
00:03:45,191 --> 00:03:46,290
(The maximum that they can receive through voting is 20 dollars.)

92
00:03:46,291 --> 00:03:48,090
If they're going to set a price,

93
00:03:48,091 --> 00:03:51,230
it'd be better to create a dish...

94
00:03:51,231 --> 00:03:52,561
that's worth paying a high price.

95
00:03:52,601 --> 00:03:54,970
Among all the food I've eaten in my life,

96
00:03:54,971 --> 00:03:57,271
what was the most expensive dish?

97
00:03:59,041 --> 00:04:00,571
How about jeongabok?

98
00:04:02,241 --> 00:04:03,511
How can I make jeongabok?

99
00:04:03,841 --> 00:04:04,911
It's easy to make.

100
00:04:05,311 --> 00:04:06,710
It's expensive because it uses...

101
00:04:06,711 --> 00:04:08,250
many valuable ingredients.

102
00:04:08,251 --> 00:04:09,311
This is about pricing.

103
00:04:09,881 --> 00:04:11,320
Right? You can't value the dish...

104
00:04:11,321 --> 00:04:12,981
at five dollars while eating this.

105
00:04:13,951 --> 00:04:16,232
Let's make something that people will pay 20 dollars for.

106
00:04:16,321 --> 00:04:19,960
When I thought about what kind of food older ladies would pay...

107
00:04:19,961 --> 00:04:21,361
a high price for,

108
00:04:21,891 --> 00:04:24,231
jeongabok came to my mind.

109
00:04:25,831 --> 00:04:29,170
(Jeongabok)

110
00:04:29,171 --> 00:04:31,040
Some places sell it for over 200 dollars.

111
00:04:31,041 --> 00:04:34,040
On average, it's around 100 dollars. It's also priced...

112
00:04:34,041 --> 00:04:35,970
between 60 and 80 dollars.

113
00:04:35,971 --> 00:04:37,340
A fancy dish...

114
00:04:37,341 --> 00:04:40,680
naturally carries a high value.

115
00:04:40,681 --> 00:04:41,981
That's why I chose jeongabok.

116
00:04:42,381 --> 00:04:44,461
Can you make jeongabok with a budget of 200 dollars?

117
00:04:44,581 --> 00:04:46,521
I was surprised. Jeongabok?

118
00:04:47,091 --> 00:04:48,991
I was impressed. It was very impactful.

119
00:04:49,851 --> 00:04:51,661
- We're leaving in five minutes.
- Okay.

120
00:04:52,791 --> 00:04:55,131
(They must buy ingredients at a nearby grocery store.)

121
00:04:56,731 --> 00:04:59,301
With glutinous rice powder...

122
00:04:59,961 --> 00:05:02,301
(Let's run.)

123
00:05:02,801 --> 00:05:05,001
- I'm going to win first place.
- The Star In My Heart.

124
00:05:06,071 --> 00:05:08,031
(Jo Seo Hyung sets out first to a grocery store.)

125
00:05:08,141 --> 00:05:09,941
- Let's hurry.
- We must hurry.

126
00:05:10,381 --> 00:05:11,840
(Additional ingredients up to 200 dollars can be purchased...)

127
00:05:11,841 --> 00:05:12,921
(at a local grocery store.)

128
00:05:13,141 --> 00:05:16,181
(A nearby local grocery store)

129
00:05:16,711 --> 00:05:18,951
(Arriving at the grocery store at 8am)

130
00:05:18,981 --> 00:05:20,650
(4 hours and 30 minutes before the business starts)

131
00:05:20,651 --> 00:05:22,571
- Let's get meat 1st.
- We're the 1st to arrive.

132
00:05:23,391 --> 00:05:24,821
Why are peeled scallions so cheap?

133
00:05:24,891 --> 00:05:26,767
- What's with the prices?
- What's going on here?

134
00:05:26,791 --> 00:05:28,951
- Two bundles should be enough.
- That will be enough.

135
00:05:29,191 --> 00:05:31,131
- Tomatoes are here.
- They have tomatoes.

136
00:05:31,261 --> 00:05:32,830
Enoki mushrooms are only 59 cents.

137
00:05:32,831 --> 00:05:34,500
- Why don't we add them for texture?
- And with this one...

138
00:05:34,501 --> 00:05:36,730
- Are fatsia shoots 80 dollars?
- Yes.

139
00:05:36,731 --> 00:05:38,771
- It's okay.
- The total is 110 dollars so far.

140
00:05:39,041 --> 00:05:40,711
- Is it 110 dollars so far?
- Yes.

141
00:05:41,671 --> 00:05:42,671
Let's go.

142
00:05:42,672 --> 00:05:44,041
(Other teams start to arrive.)

143
00:05:45,141 --> 00:05:46,641
Korean beef briskets are over here.

144
00:05:48,651 --> 00:05:50,351
Our total is only 68 dollars.

145
00:05:50,551 --> 00:05:53,721
For a 3rd of this size, can I get 1 more piece?

146
00:05:53,821 --> 00:05:55,090
Ginger for 70 cents.

147
00:05:55,091 --> 00:05:57,221
We got 3kg of shrimp and 600g of minced pork.

148
00:05:58,021 --> 00:06:00,060
(Kim Hee Eun arrived a little late.)

149
00:06:00,061 --> 00:06:01,130
We must hurry.

150
00:06:01,131 --> 00:06:02,561
We wanted to give the feeling...

151
00:06:02,861 --> 00:06:04,530
of having enjoyed a proper, satisfying meal...

152
00:06:04,531 --> 00:06:06,161
while also eating healthy.

153
00:06:07,571 --> 00:06:09,031
A type of health food.

154
00:06:10,171 --> 00:06:11,571
Shall we use abalone?

155
00:06:11,671 --> 00:06:13,171
Abalone is good.

156
00:06:13,771 --> 00:06:15,670
I wanted to make tteokgalbi...

157
00:06:15,671 --> 00:06:17,111
with abalone and fatsia shoots.

158
00:06:20,081 --> 00:06:21,081
Go.

159
00:06:22,381 --> 00:06:24,651
- We need a shopping cart.
- A shopping cart. Let's go.

160
00:06:27,521 --> 00:06:31,221
(She started by grabbing a lot of fatsia shoots.)

161
00:06:35,291 --> 00:06:36,561
- Hee Eun.
- Yes?

162
00:06:37,331 --> 00:06:39,131
There's no abalone.

163
00:06:39,431 --> 00:06:42,301
(There's no abalone.)

164
00:06:42,731 --> 00:06:43,771
They bought it all.

165
00:06:44,101 --> 00:06:45,301
We bought it all.

166
00:06:45,901 --> 00:06:47,511
It's our main ingredient.

167
00:06:51,281 --> 00:06:52,541
It's also our main ingredient.

168
00:06:54,181 --> 00:06:55,850
- How much did you buy?
- All of it.

169
00:06:55,851 --> 00:06:56,981
- All of this?
- Yes.

170
00:06:57,381 --> 00:06:58,421
We bought it first.

171
00:06:59,751 --> 00:07:01,091
It's also our main ingredient.

172
00:07:03,321 --> 00:07:04,361
Really?

173
00:07:06,761 --> 00:07:08,161
Sir, is there any abalone?

174
00:07:08,261 --> 00:07:09,361
Yes, there is.

175
00:07:10,031 --> 00:07:12,261
- I'll take all of it.
- All of it.

176
00:07:12,501 --> 00:07:14,001
Did you buy all the abalone?

177
00:07:14,571 --> 00:07:17,871
(1.5kg of abalone, 90 dollars)

178
00:07:19,971 --> 00:07:21,240
He bought it all.

179
00:07:21,241 --> 00:07:23,581
I couldn't hide how upset I was.

180
00:07:24,341 --> 00:07:26,281
That much would be perfect for us.

181
00:07:26,441 --> 00:07:28,150
- That much...
- All of it isn't enough for us.

182
00:07:28,151 --> 00:07:29,151
- It's not enough?
- No.

183
00:07:29,152 --> 00:07:31,057
- It's our main ingredient...
- What are you making?

184
00:07:31,081 --> 00:07:32,221
We're making jeongabok.

185
00:07:34,151 --> 00:07:35,191
Jeongabok?

186
00:07:35,921 --> 00:07:37,261
I can understand.

187
00:07:39,661 --> 00:07:42,531
After all, it's their last chance too.

188
00:07:43,231 --> 00:07:46,300
There are no compromises. I would've done the same anyway.

189
00:07:46,301 --> 00:07:47,801
No matter how disappointed I am,

190
00:07:48,101 --> 00:07:50,401
it's not going to make abalone magically appear.

191
00:07:50,771 --> 00:07:52,131
Let's change the plan and move on.

192
00:07:54,141 --> 00:07:55,141
Honey.

193
00:07:56,741 --> 00:07:58,857
Why don't we make something with fatsia shoots and shrimp?

194
00:07:58,881 --> 00:08:00,601
- Without abalone?
- Completely without it.

195
00:08:00,781 --> 00:08:03,351
Thankfully, in the frozen seafood section,

196
00:08:03,781 --> 00:08:07,221
I saw big and plump shrimp.

197
00:08:08,751 --> 00:08:10,271
We'll do it without abalone this time.

198
00:08:12,061 --> 00:08:13,361
This is fate.

199
00:08:15,861 --> 00:08:18,501
(A team is already at the register.)

200
00:08:18,701 --> 00:08:20,330
We'll include the ones...

201
00:08:20,331 --> 00:08:21,691
- Okay.
- that we definitely need.

202
00:08:23,001 --> 00:08:24,341
We definitely need this.

203
00:08:25,401 --> 00:08:26,841
How much is the total so far?

204
00:08:28,411 --> 00:08:29,841
(Breathing nervously)

205
00:08:30,211 --> 00:08:33,081
It's 173 dollars. Let's get one pack of squid.

206
00:08:33,851 --> 00:08:35,311
Okay, let's take this.

207
00:08:35,751 --> 00:08:37,681
- I'm sorry. It's 22 dollars.
- We apologise.

208
00:08:38,221 --> 00:08:39,251
Is that good?

209
00:08:40,551 --> 00:08:41,591
Please.

210
00:08:41,791 --> 00:08:43,520
Is that good? We're over by 28 cents.

211
00:08:43,521 --> 00:08:45,161
Which one shall we take out?

212
00:08:45,791 --> 00:08:48,290
Can we switch minced pork with something else?

213
00:08:48,291 --> 00:08:49,291
With what?

214
00:08:50,331 --> 00:08:52,500
- Oranges.
- Oranges?

215
00:08:52,501 --> 00:08:54,101
How much is minced pork?

216
00:08:55,171 --> 00:08:56,501
It's 5.4 dollars.

217
00:08:56,771 --> 00:08:59,670
When I imagine the pork flavour,

218
00:08:59,671 --> 00:09:02,810
we'll need a citrus flavour like yuzu.

219
00:09:02,811 --> 00:09:04,110
Or...

220
00:09:04,111 --> 00:09:05,741
Then we have to give up on minced pork.

221
00:09:05,881 --> 00:09:06,941
Minced pork?

222
00:09:08,411 --> 00:09:10,950
(Meanwhile, Kim Kwan Hoon is at the next register.)

223
00:09:10,951 --> 00:09:11,981
What?

224
00:09:12,781 --> 00:09:14,901
Sir, we have 50 dollars left. Please bring that here.

225
00:09:15,791 --> 00:09:17,160
- Please bring that here.
- You have 50 dollars left?

226
00:09:17,161 --> 00:09:18,437
- Could you buy this for us?
- What?

227
00:09:18,461 --> 00:09:20,060
Please buy this for us.

228
00:09:20,061 --> 00:09:22,290
Come on, you're rich. Please help me.

229
00:09:22,291 --> 00:09:25,000
I'm a businessman, so I'll buy this and sell it for a high price.

230
00:09:25,001 --> 00:09:29,031
Then I'll pay for it later. Please.

231
00:09:29,671 --> 00:09:32,100
When you add a spoonful of cheonhyehyang in any dish,

232
00:09:32,101 --> 00:09:34,070
- the taste becomes very fancy.
- Right.

233
00:09:34,071 --> 00:09:35,351
Do you want to split it in half?

234
00:09:36,441 --> 00:09:37,781
Let's split it in half.

235
00:09:39,011 --> 00:09:42,281
Does anyone need cheonhyehyang that adds a refreshing flavour?

236
00:09:42,811 --> 00:09:44,951
It's only 6 dollars. The entire pack is 12.5 dollars.

237
00:09:45,681 --> 00:09:47,291
We can buy it and share it in half.

238
00:09:49,621 --> 00:09:52,361
(No one is interested.)

239
00:09:52,861 --> 00:09:54,991
Excuse me, can I buy only half of it?

240
00:09:55,931 --> 00:09:58,331
Or is it possible to buy them individually?

241
00:09:58,931 --> 00:10:01,030
- Yes, it's two dollars for each.
- Is it possible?

242
00:10:01,031 --> 00:10:02,731
It's two dollars for each? That's great.

243
00:10:03,201 --> 00:10:04,941
We should take these out...

244
00:10:05,141 --> 00:10:06,771
and buy this and that instead.

245
00:10:07,071 --> 00:10:08,710
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

246
00:10:08,711 --> 00:10:10,710
- I'm sorry for causing trouble.
- I'm sorry.

247
00:10:10,711 --> 00:10:13,411
- Have a good day. Goodbye.
- Thank you.

248
00:10:13,951 --> 00:10:16,751
Once we get there, we'll start by blanching fatsia shoots.

249
00:10:17,181 --> 00:10:19,150
(Until the business starts)

250
00:10:19,151 --> 00:10:21,251
(3 hours and 30 minutes)

251
00:10:21,891 --> 00:10:23,790
(The side dish specialist gets ready.)

252
00:10:23,791 --> 00:10:25,261
Shall we start our business?

253
00:10:26,121 --> 00:10:29,791
I grew up in the mountains of Gangwon Province.

254
00:10:30,261 --> 00:10:32,001
Back home,

255
00:10:32,461 --> 00:10:34,331
we rarely use sesame oil.

256
00:10:34,431 --> 00:10:36,971
Almost everything is prepared with perilla oil.

257
00:10:38,271 --> 00:10:41,041
For the fatsia shoots, I lightly blanched them...

258
00:10:41,441 --> 00:10:43,317
and dressed them with perilla oil, green plum syrup,

259
00:10:43,341 --> 00:10:45,840
and a touch of soy sauce.

260
00:10:45,841 --> 00:10:47,857
(Sesame seeds, soy sauce, green plum syrup, perilla oil)

261
00:10:47,881 --> 00:10:49,580
(Seasoned fatsia shoots with perilla oil)

262
00:10:49,581 --> 00:10:52,251
(Gangwon Province-style)

263
00:10:52,421 --> 00:10:54,621
Most of the other chefs specialise in Western cuisine.

264
00:10:55,551 --> 00:10:58,591
There was no other Korean cuisine chef.

265
00:10:58,991 --> 00:11:00,791
When I saw the guests' age group,

266
00:11:00,961 --> 00:11:04,331
I expected some of them to find the meal too heavy.

267
00:11:04,531 --> 00:11:06,460
I hope that they'll enjoy our tomato kimchi...

268
00:11:06,461 --> 00:11:07,770
whenever the food gets heavy.

269
00:11:07,771 --> 00:11:10,101
We have quite a few with bright acidity.

270
00:11:10,641 --> 00:11:13,000
Older ladies will like it. It's a type of kimchi...

271
00:11:13,001 --> 00:11:14,941
that you don't eat every day.

272
00:11:15,671 --> 00:11:17,980
Like the seasoning for cucumber kimchi,

273
00:11:17,981 --> 00:11:20,080
I added chives and regular kimchi seasoning.

274
00:11:20,081 --> 00:11:23,381
(Also adding a broth made with anchovies and vegetables)

275
00:11:23,651 --> 00:11:25,221
By using Daejeo tomatoes,

276
00:11:25,351 --> 00:11:27,191
I made tomato kimchi.

277
00:11:28,051 --> 00:11:31,921
(Refreshing tomato kimchi to cleanse the palate)

278
00:11:32,791 --> 00:11:35,191
I'm sure they like seafood.

279
00:11:36,331 --> 00:11:38,800
(The side dish specialist's secret touch)

280
00:11:38,801 --> 00:11:41,531
(Cheonhyehyang)

281
00:11:41,631 --> 00:11:43,941
I'm going to make a sauce with cheonhyehyang.

282
00:11:44,771 --> 00:11:46,440
Cheonhyehyang is finely blended...

283
00:11:46,441 --> 00:11:49,511
and combined with a touch of mustard and soy sauce for soup.

284
00:11:50,171 --> 00:11:52,611
I made a piquant and savoury sauce.

285
00:11:53,141 --> 00:11:55,150
(Crunchy pear)

286
00:11:55,151 --> 00:11:58,051
(Blanched webfoot octopus, cheonhyehyang sauce)

287
00:11:58,381 --> 00:11:59,920
It looks beautiful.

288
00:11:59,921 --> 00:12:01,721
(Making it sweet with fragrant cheonhyehyang)

289
00:12:01,851 --> 00:12:03,350
The cheonhyehyang fragrance is very strong.

290
00:12:03,351 --> 00:12:06,021
(Cheonhyehyang webfoot octopus salad)

291
00:12:06,291 --> 00:12:08,361
- This is insane.
- It looks so beautiful.

292
00:12:08,791 --> 00:12:10,861
Ideas kept coming to me.

293
00:12:11,261 --> 00:12:14,131
If you're from Gangwon Province, you can't resist tofu.

294
00:12:16,031 --> 00:12:18,270
(Tofu jeon with zucchini and chilli strip)

295
00:12:18,271 --> 00:12:19,901
For the grilled pork belly,

296
00:12:20,071 --> 00:12:22,041
I added a touch of doenjang...

297
00:12:22,241 --> 00:12:24,210
to reduce the sweetness.

298
00:12:24,211 --> 00:12:28,281
(Grilled doenjang marinated pork)

299
00:12:30,281 --> 00:12:34,520
(Stir-fried perilla seeds and mushrooms)

300
00:12:34,521 --> 00:12:38,221
(Seasoned scallions with anchovies)

301
00:12:38,321 --> 00:12:40,761
I focused on the everyday side dishes...

302
00:12:40,831 --> 00:12:43,391
that I ate often when I lived in Gangwon Province.

303
00:12:43,691 --> 00:12:46,530
How many side dishes do we make in a single day?

304
00:12:46,531 --> 00:12:48,071
I'm not exaggerating.

305
00:12:48,401 --> 00:12:50,771
We make about 500 to 600 portions.

306
00:12:51,601 --> 00:12:54,241
Making a wide variety of side dishes in a short amount of time.

307
00:12:55,371 --> 00:12:57,211
That's where my strength lies.

308
00:12:57,481 --> 00:13:00,080
One, two, three, four, five, six, seven.

309
00:13:00,081 --> 00:13:01,551
- We have 7.
- We have 7?

310
00:13:02,281 --> 00:13:06,221
(7 side dishes made in an instant)

311
00:13:08,491 --> 00:13:11,491
Turning everyday side dish into a dish.

312
00:13:12,161 --> 00:13:13,261
Who created this menu?

313
00:13:13,491 --> 00:13:15,231
Please bring her out.

314
00:13:16,491 --> 00:13:17,601
Bring her out.

315
00:13:17,701 --> 00:13:20,630
Other teams will probably compete with one main dish.

316
00:13:20,631 --> 00:13:22,800
This is what makes us different.

317
00:13:22,801 --> 00:13:24,601
What goes well with rice is side dishes.

318
00:13:25,801 --> 00:13:28,171
A plate this small can't hold everything I want to show.

319
00:13:30,241 --> 00:13:31,710
- Over here?
- Yes.

320
00:13:31,711 --> 00:13:33,211
- Right there.
- On top?

321
00:13:35,381 --> 00:13:37,050
(100 servings of 7 side dishes...)

322
00:13:37,051 --> 00:13:40,281
(created by the hands and flavours of Gangwon Province)

323
00:13:41,991 --> 00:13:44,321
(Are you exhausted?)

324
00:13:44,561 --> 00:13:45,661
I'm very tired.

325
00:13:46,721 --> 00:13:49,081
Should we blend strawberries and make something refreshing?

326
00:13:49,861 --> 00:13:52,530
I used this blender earlier for the sauce.

327
00:13:52,531 --> 00:13:53,930
It makes everything very smooth.

328
00:13:53,931 --> 00:13:56,501
For the blueberries, I'll leave a little texture.

329
00:13:57,471 --> 00:14:00,571
(Adding whole frozen strawberries)

330
00:14:01,271 --> 00:14:02,370
"Smoothie."

331
00:14:02,371 --> 00:14:04,181
(Set it to smoothie mode and press start.)

332
00:14:06,181 --> 00:14:08,181
It's surprisingly quiet and blends so smoothly.

333
00:14:10,311 --> 00:14:11,880
There's no need to strain it. It's very fine.

334
00:14:11,881 --> 00:14:14,190
- It's like slush.
- Yes.

335
00:14:14,191 --> 00:14:16,021
- Didn't it turn out so soft?
- Yes.

336
00:14:18,121 --> 00:14:19,891
- It's like slush.
- Exactly.

337
00:14:21,961 --> 00:14:23,891
It's nice and refreshing.

338
00:14:26,931 --> 00:14:28,031
Okay.

339
00:14:28,801 --> 00:14:30,741
What's important is getting the groceries fast.

340
00:14:33,401 --> 00:14:35,411
The moment I saw the rice,

341
00:14:36,241 --> 00:14:39,141
that dish was the first thing that came to mind.

342
00:14:41,381 --> 00:14:42,451
Rice...

343
00:14:42,911 --> 00:14:44,451
We have to make twenty plates.

344
00:14:45,481 --> 00:14:47,591
Without relying on strong artificial seasoning,

345
00:14:47,891 --> 00:14:49,650
I kept thinking about...

346
00:14:49,651 --> 00:14:51,391
what would taste good with rice.

347
00:14:53,821 --> 00:14:55,260
Like yuxiang shiitake mushrooms.

348
00:14:55,261 --> 00:14:57,331
I don't mean the sauce, but the mushrooms.

349
00:14:58,131 --> 00:14:59,360
- Yes.
- For the elders.

350
00:14:59,361 --> 00:15:01,771
It's stylish and tasty.

351
00:15:02,371 --> 00:15:03,531
Talk softly.

352
00:15:04,541 --> 00:15:07,541
Yuxiang, which means fish fragrance, doesn't mean the dish uses fish.

353
00:15:07,911 --> 00:15:10,447
It refers to the dishes which use spicy, sweet, and sour sauce...

354
00:15:10,471 --> 00:15:13,311
that was initially used to cook fish with.

355
00:15:13,741 --> 00:15:16,011
Cheorwon is known for its shiitake mushrooms too.

356
00:15:16,851 --> 00:15:17,951
Fresh shiitake mushrooms...

357
00:15:18,921 --> 00:15:21,121
- They should have them, right?
- I think so.

358
00:15:21,621 --> 00:15:22,851
That's why we need to hurry.

359
00:15:24,061 --> 00:15:26,541
- We must find shiitake mushrooms.
- Shiitake mushrooms first.

360
00:15:27,021 --> 00:15:28,791
If we can't find shiitake mushrooms,

361
00:15:29,631 --> 00:15:30,731
that changes things.

362
00:15:39,541 --> 00:15:40,541
Shiitake...

363
00:15:44,511 --> 00:15:45,511
(Exclaiming)

364
00:15:45,781 --> 00:15:47,981
Here they are.

365
00:15:48,711 --> 00:15:50,280
(Shiitake mushrooms, 1.98 dollars)

366
00:15:50,281 --> 00:15:51,780
It's one of the most impactful shiitake mushrooms...

367
00:15:51,781 --> 00:15:53,681
I've seen in my life.

368
00:15:54,291 --> 00:15:55,350
I'm not lying.

369
00:15:55,351 --> 00:15:58,321
I could smell them from 1m to 2m away.

370
00:15:59,221 --> 00:16:00,661
These mushrooms are insane.

371
00:16:02,091 --> 00:16:04,101
If we get 90, that's around 89 dollars.

372
00:16:06,061 --> 00:16:08,401
Let's check the price.

373
00:16:09,201 --> 00:16:11,601
(Shiitake mushrooms)

374
00:16:12,241 --> 00:16:14,311
We calculated meticulously.

375
00:16:15,111 --> 00:16:17,481
(After a thorough calculation)

376
00:16:17,841 --> 00:16:18,981
- Let's go.
- Okay.

377
00:16:20,281 --> 00:16:23,211
We might not have enough time to prepare.

378
00:16:23,511 --> 00:16:25,981
- I won't be complacent.
- Right. It'll be really hectic.

379
00:16:26,521 --> 00:16:29,051
- I'm desperate.
- Me too.

380
00:16:30,021 --> 00:16:32,561
For me, the memories...

381
00:16:33,461 --> 00:16:37,501
of the 2nd half of Round 2 remain as a big shock to me.

382
00:16:38,061 --> 00:16:39,861
(Can you make more?)

383
00:16:40,331 --> 00:16:43,570
(During the second half, he hurriedly cooked more hot pot.)

384
00:16:43,571 --> 00:16:46,070
I've been cooking for a very long time,

385
00:16:46,071 --> 00:16:48,811
and I prided myself in cooking for the customers.

386
00:16:49,611 --> 00:16:52,941
(With the food burned, they had to make multiple refunds.)

387
00:16:53,411 --> 00:16:55,250
I beat myself up for not being attentive...

388
00:16:55,251 --> 00:16:57,111
until the end.

389
00:16:57,481 --> 00:16:59,450
(I'm so ashamed.)

390
00:16:59,451 --> 00:17:01,421
Regardless of the result,

391
00:17:02,351 --> 00:17:03,871
I should do this well until the end...

392
00:17:03,951 --> 00:17:07,361
so it'll help me improve with my cooking in the future.

393
00:17:07,861 --> 00:17:08,961
I'll do well.

394
00:17:09,361 --> 00:17:10,961
- I'm reflecting on myself.
- Right.

395
00:17:11,661 --> 00:17:13,461
This could become our last round.

396
00:17:15,701 --> 00:17:18,241
I'll stir-fry the vegetables with the sauce by 12pm.

397
00:17:18,841 --> 00:17:20,541
We'll prepare everything in advance...

398
00:17:21,411 --> 00:17:22,941
so we can have it easy.

399
00:17:23,341 --> 00:17:26,541
This time, we'll only serve perfect food.

400
00:17:28,081 --> 00:17:29,281
I'll remove the stems.

401
00:17:30,851 --> 00:17:31,881
Okay.

402
00:17:32,351 --> 00:17:34,071
I can smell shiitake from all the way here.

403
00:17:34,851 --> 00:17:36,290
Fresh shiitake mushrooms smell amazing,

404
00:17:36,291 --> 00:17:38,011
and I want the customers to taste it fully.

405
00:17:38,321 --> 00:17:39,991
The mushrooms look delicious as is.

406
00:17:40,761 --> 00:17:42,931
They just can't taste bad.

407
00:17:44,231 --> 00:17:45,961
Shall we score the mushrooms first?

408
00:17:46,031 --> 00:17:47,831
- I'll do it. You should mince it.
- Okay.

409
00:17:49,301 --> 00:17:53,771
You add shrimp paste into shiitake mushrooms and deep-fry it.

410
00:17:55,671 --> 00:17:57,511
One with coarsely-chopped shrimps...

411
00:17:58,811 --> 00:18:00,451
and the other with finely-minced shrimps.

412
00:18:02,281 --> 00:18:04,751
We prepared two different textures.

413
00:18:05,921 --> 00:18:07,551
For additional texture,

414
00:18:08,491 --> 00:18:09,651
we used burdock...

415
00:18:10,151 --> 00:18:11,261
and shiitake mushrooms.

416
00:18:14,191 --> 00:18:16,361
Ginger and green onions will add to the flavour.

417
00:18:17,931 --> 00:18:20,361
I mixed them all as if kneading.

418
00:18:22,071 --> 00:18:24,941
(The shrimp paste enhances both the flavour and the texture.)

419
00:18:25,241 --> 00:18:27,041
- Let's shape one together.
- Together? Okay.

420
00:18:27,941 --> 00:18:29,011
Put it in.

421
00:18:29,811 --> 00:18:31,440
Make sure you don't trap any air.

422
00:18:31,441 --> 00:18:32,481
Okay.

423
00:18:33,611 --> 00:18:36,151
- Press it down flat.
- Press it?

424
00:18:37,851 --> 00:18:39,551
- Is this too much?
- It's fine.

425
00:18:40,751 --> 00:18:42,051
It will soak in anyway.

426
00:18:42,751 --> 00:18:43,751
Okay.

427
00:18:45,721 --> 00:18:47,161
How long does it take to steam them?

428
00:18:47,361 --> 00:18:48,661
Seven minutes.

429
00:18:49,691 --> 00:18:50,901
We steamed them first.

430
00:18:52,001 --> 00:18:53,731
We cut down on the deep-frying time...

431
00:18:55,871 --> 00:18:58,071
to keep the taste light.

432
00:18:59,201 --> 00:19:02,411
Let's think about how we can make it visually attractive.

433
00:19:02,541 --> 00:19:04,341
I thought of one.

434
00:19:04,681 --> 00:19:06,740
We can't make the batter in advance.

435
00:19:06,741 --> 00:19:08,151
- It'll be real-time.
- Yes.

436
00:19:08,381 --> 00:19:10,811
It'll be less crispy because it's not fermented enough.

437
00:19:11,021 --> 00:19:13,420
It'll be good to deep-fry rice,

438
00:19:13,421 --> 00:19:15,050
blend it, and add it to the batter.

439
00:19:15,051 --> 00:19:16,150
(The frying batter mixed with deep-fried ground rice)

440
00:19:16,151 --> 00:19:17,661
To add the crispy texture,

441
00:19:17,821 --> 00:19:20,961
I thought about adding rice to the batter.

442
00:19:21,791 --> 00:19:23,231
I'll deep-fry just this much rice.

443
00:19:24,331 --> 00:19:25,331
Okay.

444
00:19:25,861 --> 00:19:28,031
Deep-fried rice would taste savoury.

445
00:19:28,931 --> 00:19:32,201
I'll add that flavour to the batter, no matter how little it may be,

446
00:19:33,801 --> 00:19:36,481
so that the overall taste connects to the flavour of rice naturally.

447
00:19:37,981 --> 00:19:40,711
(He enhances the flavour by deep-frying rice.)

448
00:19:41,451 --> 00:19:44,481
(He blends it finely to turn it into ground rice.)

449
00:19:44,721 --> 00:19:45,820
Ground rice.

450
00:19:45,821 --> 00:19:47,461
It's not steamed rice with a random dish.

451
00:19:47,651 --> 00:19:49,821
It's steamed rice with rice-flavoured dish.

452
00:19:51,961 --> 00:19:54,361
So the flavour connects naturally.

453
00:19:55,991 --> 00:19:58,831
(Deep-fry steamed mushrooms to complete it.)

454
00:19:59,461 --> 00:20:00,761
As crispy as possible.

455
00:20:01,971 --> 00:20:03,411
While retaining the mushroom flavour.

456
00:20:10,171 --> 00:20:11,941
It's done. You can taste it now.

457
00:20:15,951 --> 00:20:17,150
Will your mom be moved when she eats this?

458
00:20:17,151 --> 00:20:18,551
Yes.

459
00:20:18,881 --> 00:20:21,050
The shrimp flavour and the texture are nice.

460
00:20:21,051 --> 00:20:22,927
(Deep-fried mushrooms have turned out to be crispy.)

461
00:20:22,951 --> 00:20:24,490
The juiciness of shrimps...

462
00:20:24,491 --> 00:20:26,821
and the chewiness and softness of shiitake mushrooms.

463
00:20:28,591 --> 00:20:31,661
(Yuxiang sauce whets your appetite.)

464
00:20:32,331 --> 00:20:35,101
Sweet, sour, and spicy yuxiang sauce.

465
00:20:36,831 --> 00:20:39,901
I thought they would harmonize nicely.

466
00:20:41,871 --> 00:20:43,911
This must have a strong impact.

467
00:20:46,511 --> 00:20:48,980
I just hoped that the customers would think the same.

468
00:20:48,981 --> 00:20:50,927
(Yuxiang shiitake mushrooms using Odae Rice batter)

469
00:20:50,951 --> 00:20:52,950
I'll move the hearts of the customers...

470
00:20:52,951 --> 00:20:54,551
and survive the revival match.

471
00:20:55,021 --> 00:20:56,051
Good luck to us.

472
00:20:57,491 --> 00:20:58,621
The neighbour seniors.

473
00:20:58,921 --> 00:21:01,391
- I didn't expect them to be women.
- Me neither.

474
00:21:01,691 --> 00:21:03,391
Do they farm rice?

475
00:21:04,361 --> 00:21:07,001
It could be fun. Like,

476
00:21:07,561 --> 00:21:09,971
"They created such different texture using our rice."

477
00:21:10,901 --> 00:21:13,441
Since we're using rice,

478
00:21:13,771 --> 00:21:16,070
let's give it the Provence vibe.

479
00:21:16,071 --> 00:21:17,071
I see.

480
00:21:17,671 --> 00:21:18,880
(Cooking a French cuisine for the revival match as well)

481
00:21:18,881 --> 00:21:21,481
When I saw the rice,

482
00:21:21,811 --> 00:21:24,311
I thought of Provence right away.

483
00:21:25,621 --> 00:21:27,790
(Paris, Provence)

484
00:21:27,791 --> 00:21:29,591
That's where rice is harvested.

485
00:21:30,421 --> 00:21:31,820
The red rice...

486
00:21:31,821 --> 00:21:34,561
called Camargue is popular in Provence.

487
00:21:34,931 --> 00:21:36,991
Of course, the rice we're using...

488
00:21:37,491 --> 00:21:39,960
has a very different texture to the rice from Provence.

489
00:21:39,961 --> 00:21:41,847
(Korean rice is smooth and sticky. European rice is less sticky.)

490
00:21:41,871 --> 00:21:43,330
Even though I am using Korean rice,

491
00:21:43,331 --> 00:21:45,240
I wanted the texture to come off completely different,

492
00:21:45,241 --> 00:21:47,241
as if I am using a totally different kind of rice.

493
00:21:47,541 --> 00:21:49,110
So the name of the dish is...

494
00:21:49,111 --> 00:21:51,711
Healthy Provence-style Vegetables Pot Rice.

495
00:21:53,881 --> 00:21:55,361
French cuisine wouldn't work on them.

496
00:21:56,351 --> 00:21:57,451
Who knows?

497
00:21:57,711 --> 00:21:59,951
They could be elegant elderly ladies.

498
00:22:00,951 --> 00:22:04,991
Elegant or not, would this suit their taste?

499
00:22:06,821 --> 00:22:08,301
Hoon, did you start cooking the rice?

500
00:22:08,461 --> 00:22:09,991
I'm going to start now.

501
00:22:12,431 --> 00:22:15,731
(Raw rice)

502
00:22:16,401 --> 00:22:20,271
(The raw rice enters the oven.)

503
00:22:26,581 --> 00:22:29,581
(While the raw rice is roasted...)

504
00:22:29,781 --> 00:22:31,920
- I'll boil the water for the broth.
- Okay.

505
00:22:31,921 --> 00:22:34,021
(Vegetables stock to cook the rice with)

506
00:22:37,551 --> 00:22:39,261
The rice should be roasted by now.

507
00:22:40,591 --> 00:22:42,661
(Checking on the rice which was left in the oven)

508
00:22:44,561 --> 00:22:46,401
(Golden brown)

509
00:22:46,531 --> 00:22:48,371
With the rice we know,

510
00:22:48,501 --> 00:22:51,841
we can't make the fluffy texture.

511
00:22:52,741 --> 00:22:56,041
(The roasted rice goes straight into the rice cooker.)

512
00:22:58,181 --> 00:23:00,111
I added vegetables like onions, green onions,

513
00:23:00,211 --> 00:23:02,851
kabocha squash, and mushrooms after stir-frying them in olive oil.

514
00:23:04,011 --> 00:23:05,621
Can I add Korean wild chives too?

515
00:23:05,681 --> 00:23:07,120
Just add them. We have some left.

516
00:23:07,121 --> 00:23:08,121
Okay.

517
00:23:08,122 --> 00:23:09,950
The fragrance of Korean wild chives...

518
00:23:09,951 --> 00:23:13,020
will play a role as important as...

519
00:23:13,021 --> 00:23:14,831
adding other herbs.

520
00:23:16,091 --> 00:23:20,831
(Roasted rice, various vegetables, and vegetable stock)

521
00:23:21,731 --> 00:23:24,301
The kelp became soft under the sunlight.

522
00:23:25,271 --> 00:23:27,141
I'm behind you. Rice incoming.

523
00:23:28,911 --> 00:23:32,211
(Put it into the rice cooker, and the pot rice is ready.)

524
00:23:33,011 --> 00:23:35,281
I chose pork shoulder.

525
00:23:36,551 --> 00:23:39,951
I'll confit pork shoulder in oil.

526
00:23:41,991 --> 00:23:44,050
(Confit: A French cooking method of putting ingredients into oil...)

527
00:23:44,051 --> 00:23:45,761
(and cooking it slowly in low heat)

528
00:23:47,461 --> 00:23:50,261
(You must check the temperature frequently.)

529
00:23:50,731 --> 00:23:52,461
The fire didn't extinguish, did it?

530
00:23:54,701 --> 00:23:56,131
- Now...
- Can you see it?

531
00:23:56,901 --> 00:23:58,701
- I think it's turned off.
- Right?

532
00:24:06,641 --> 00:24:07,641
There you go.

533
00:24:07,642 --> 00:24:08,651
(Blaze up)

534
00:24:12,381 --> 00:24:13,821
The flame kept going out.

535
00:24:14,081 --> 00:24:15,791
You need to maintain a very low heat.

536
00:24:16,691 --> 00:24:19,561
When I reduced the heat a tad bit, the wind kept blowing out the fire.

537
00:24:21,861 --> 00:24:25,331
(Adding kindlings)

538
00:24:26,331 --> 00:24:29,071
(Failed)

539
00:24:29,901 --> 00:24:31,101
I can't see it.

540
00:24:31,301 --> 00:24:33,541
That was a challenge.

541
00:24:34,611 --> 00:24:37,011
(Oven)

542
00:24:43,811 --> 00:24:45,780
(He removes the oven tray.)

543
00:24:45,781 --> 00:24:46,821
My gosh.

544
00:24:47,351 --> 00:24:48,750
I compared the size of the pot with the oven next to it,

545
00:24:48,751 --> 00:24:50,421
and it looked like the pot would fit.

546
00:24:56,291 --> 00:24:58,301
So I put the whole thing into the oven.

547
00:25:00,061 --> 00:25:02,671
(The pot fits into the oven nicely.)

548
00:25:03,071 --> 00:25:04,701
Be careful when you take it out.

549
00:25:08,811 --> 00:25:12,011
As a result, I could cook it more stably.

550
00:25:13,341 --> 00:25:14,341
Done.

551
00:25:16,311 --> 00:25:19,081
(Lim Ki Hak's improvisation)

552
00:25:19,451 --> 00:25:21,391
(While the meat is getting cooked in the oven, )

553
00:25:21,891 --> 00:25:24,691
(he completes the ratatouille that will be put onto the rice.)

554
00:25:25,291 --> 00:25:29,061
(Opening the rice cooker)

555
00:25:29,761 --> 00:25:31,130
It's cooked well.

556
00:25:31,131 --> 00:25:32,230
It's fluffy.

557
00:25:32,231 --> 00:25:33,437
(Like the French rice, the rice is fluffy.)

558
00:25:33,461 --> 00:25:35,330
It's delicious.

559
00:25:35,331 --> 00:25:36,631
- Is it all cooked?
- Yes.

560
00:25:38,301 --> 00:25:39,900
"It's not the rice we know."

561
00:25:39,901 --> 00:25:41,410
Wouldn't that be a fun experience if they say like that?

562
00:25:41,411 --> 00:25:42,541
I agree.

563
00:25:43,141 --> 00:25:44,541
That'd be a success.

564
00:25:45,441 --> 00:25:47,911
It should be thick like porridge.

565
00:25:48,681 --> 00:25:50,080
They're elderly.

566
00:25:50,081 --> 00:25:51,111
I know, but...

567
00:25:51,451 --> 00:25:52,821
It's salty for seniors.

568
00:25:53,851 --> 00:25:55,990
I'm planning to make a risotto which the seniors can enjoy too.

569
00:25:55,991 --> 00:25:59,291
Something which isn't too greasy or heavy.

570
00:26:00,121 --> 00:26:01,990
Since it's spring,

571
00:26:01,991 --> 00:26:04,900
we wanted to make a fresh risotto...

572
00:26:04,901 --> 00:26:06,701
with seafood.

573
00:26:07,461 --> 00:26:09,231
We really need to showcase our pasta.

574
00:26:09,771 --> 00:26:12,241
We'll show them our pasta in the next mission.

575
00:26:13,001 --> 00:26:15,940
We cook Italian cuisine,

576
00:26:15,941 --> 00:26:17,821
and our restaurant is well-known for our pasta.

577
00:26:19,941 --> 00:26:22,810
But we had to cook using rice for this mission,

578
00:26:22,811 --> 00:26:24,451
so we boldly excluded pasta.

579
00:26:24,751 --> 00:26:25,780
I really want to survive and showcase our pasta...

580
00:26:25,781 --> 00:26:27,621
in the next mission.

581
00:26:27,681 --> 00:26:29,051
I can't get eliminated like this.

582
00:26:31,021 --> 00:26:32,741
Please allow me to continue to participate.

583
00:26:33,061 --> 00:26:35,291
(The perfect restaurant name for the mission)

584
00:26:35,461 --> 00:26:36,861
We'll definitely make it.

585
00:26:37,191 --> 00:26:40,330
(The young owners are spirited.)

586
00:26:40,331 --> 00:26:43,600
With the resolve to cook the dishes as delicious as the grandma's,

587
00:26:43,601 --> 00:26:45,041
we came up with the name.

588
00:26:45,771 --> 00:26:47,941
- They're very elderly.
- Oh, no.

589
00:26:49,311 --> 00:26:50,910
- What should we do?
- This...

590
00:26:50,911 --> 00:26:52,471
- In their 60s and 70s?
- We're doomed.

591
00:26:52,711 --> 00:26:53,711
Hey.

592
00:26:54,241 --> 00:26:55,811
A military unit is better.

593
00:26:55,981 --> 00:26:58,221
- This is bad.
- A military unit is better. This...

594
00:26:58,681 --> 00:27:01,551
There's a high chance we're going to a military unit.

595
00:27:02,121 --> 00:27:04,890
(The couple was very sure they were going to a military unit.)

596
00:27:04,891 --> 00:27:06,790
Soldiers nowadays are young,

597
00:27:06,791 --> 00:27:08,191
so they'd like western cuisine too.

598
00:27:08,291 --> 00:27:10,961
They'll like tasty, strong-flavoured food.

599
00:27:11,031 --> 00:27:13,331
We can't go with the sauce I thought of.

600
00:27:14,131 --> 00:27:15,971
It's too greasy and strong.

601
00:27:18,741 --> 00:27:20,241
After discovering the age range,

602
00:27:20,371 --> 00:27:21,741
we changed everything.

603
00:27:22,541 --> 00:27:26,840
We were thinking about a strong and straightforward flavour.

604
00:27:26,841 --> 00:27:31,181
But after seeing that their ages ranged from 60s to 70s...

605
00:27:31,451 --> 00:27:34,080
(Strong flavour out)

606
00:27:34,081 --> 00:27:35,620
(Greasiness out)

607
00:27:35,621 --> 00:27:38,820
We were going to throw in tons of meat and seafood.

608
00:27:38,821 --> 00:27:40,990
But I don't think that's suitable.

609
00:27:40,991 --> 00:27:43,131
We scrapped everything we planned.

610
00:27:43,461 --> 00:27:45,331
Let's rack our brains quickly.

611
00:27:45,601 --> 00:27:46,631
This is bad.

612
00:27:46,831 --> 00:27:47,900
Let's discuss.

613
00:27:47,901 --> 00:27:49,530
It's very different from what we expected.

614
00:27:49,531 --> 00:27:51,230
It's so hard to cook for seniors.

615
00:27:51,231 --> 00:27:53,831
(Don't Tell Grandma's quest to captivate the seniors' taste buds)

616
00:27:55,441 --> 00:27:56,541
Goodness.

617
00:27:58,781 --> 00:28:00,711
I'm only good at making broth.

618
00:28:02,011 --> 00:28:03,850
All of our dishes...

619
00:28:03,851 --> 00:28:05,351
require a lot of broth.

620
00:28:06,181 --> 00:28:08,850
Mild and light flavour.

621
00:28:08,851 --> 00:28:10,367
(Customization 1. Make it light with seafood broth.)

622
00:28:10,391 --> 00:28:14,290
I added crabs, seashells, and mussels,

623
00:28:14,291 --> 00:28:15,731
and kept boiling it.

624
00:28:19,031 --> 00:28:20,861
We cook broth yet again today.

625
00:28:25,101 --> 00:28:27,801
Season it lightly, and don't add too much butter and cheese.

626
00:28:27,871 --> 00:28:28,871
Okay.

627
00:28:29,411 --> 00:28:31,441
Always milder than what you usually prefer.

628
00:28:31,611 --> 00:28:32,940
- Okay.
- Two levels lower.

629
00:28:32,941 --> 00:28:34,617
(Customization 2. Make sure it's less salty!)

630
00:28:34,641 --> 00:28:37,311
The teams which cook strong-flavoured food will lose.

631
00:28:39,321 --> 00:28:40,651
It does smell good.

632
00:28:41,251 --> 00:28:43,020
But just a bit. The seasoning...

633
00:28:43,021 --> 00:28:46,221
(Finding the best seasoning by tasting it nonstop)

634
00:28:48,121 --> 00:28:49,131
Isn't it too bland?

635
00:28:49,261 --> 00:28:50,391
- No.
- No?

636
00:28:50,661 --> 00:28:52,800
- Okay, I'll stop here.
- Yes, let's go.

637
00:28:52,801 --> 00:28:53,801
Okay.

638
00:28:54,061 --> 00:28:55,131
Nice.

639
00:28:55,531 --> 00:28:58,501
Actually, people have it as al dente in Italy.

640
00:28:59,341 --> 00:29:02,611
But for the seniors, we'll cook it a lot longer.

641
00:29:03,641 --> 00:29:06,981
(Customization 3. Cook the rice for long so it's easy to eat.)

642
00:29:08,781 --> 00:29:10,510
It tastes fresh,

643
00:29:10,511 --> 00:29:12,121
so I was sure they'd like it.

644
00:29:12,381 --> 00:29:16,090
And it has 4 types of seafood, not just 1.

645
00:29:16,091 --> 00:29:17,720
The seafood we used...

646
00:29:17,721 --> 00:29:20,820
are abalones, webfoot octopus, and shrimps.

647
00:29:20,821 --> 00:29:22,060
(Abalones, flounder, webfoot octopus, shrimps)

648
00:29:22,061 --> 00:29:24,630
Fatsia shoots taste really good when grilled in oil.

649
00:29:24,631 --> 00:29:27,400
I placed grilled fatsia shoots on the risotto.

650
00:29:27,401 --> 00:29:30,401
(Flounder, fatsia shoots, abalone, webfoot octopus, shrimp)

651
00:29:30,871 --> 00:29:32,501
Just one dish will decide the winners.

652
00:29:32,571 --> 00:29:34,041
We must pour our everything into it.

653
00:29:35,441 --> 00:29:37,510
We're second to none when it comes to the taste,

654
00:29:37,511 --> 00:29:38,951
but that doesn't secure our victory.

655
00:29:39,381 --> 00:29:40,711
We're almost done.

656
00:29:41,281 --> 00:29:43,411
- I'm sure they'll enjoy this.
- I'll put one there.

657
00:29:44,711 --> 00:29:46,880
As long as they're served as we've prepared,

658
00:29:46,881 --> 00:29:48,420
they would like it. Right?

659
00:29:48,421 --> 00:29:50,781
(Spring Vegetables Risotto by Don't Tell Grandma is ready.)

660
00:29:53,621 --> 00:29:55,561
Do I need to make the side dishes using rice?

661
00:29:56,461 --> 00:29:57,630
(Everything About Tteokbokki)

662
00:29:57,631 --> 00:30:01,261
(And this man here is full of concerns.)

663
00:30:01,631 --> 00:30:03,200
I only know how to make tteokbokki,

664
00:30:03,201 --> 00:30:06,541
and I'm only good at making tteokbokki.

665
00:30:08,171 --> 00:30:09,171
(A great restaurant always comes back with rice.)

666
00:30:09,172 --> 00:30:10,240
(When the mission was revealed)

667
00:30:10,241 --> 00:30:11,270
What's this?

668
00:30:11,271 --> 00:30:12,711
Are we only using rice?

669
00:30:13,411 --> 00:30:15,687
Since you make tteokbokki, will you make your own rice cake?

670
00:30:15,711 --> 00:30:16,881
How can I make it using that?

671
00:30:18,381 --> 00:30:19,421
Rice?

672
00:30:19,951 --> 00:30:22,550
The dishes I can cook...

673
00:30:22,551 --> 00:30:23,750
are street food and tteokbokki.

674
00:30:23,751 --> 00:30:25,721
(He did not expect the mission.)

675
00:30:25,791 --> 00:30:27,221
Here...

676
00:30:28,861 --> 00:30:30,791
(Mumbling)

677
00:30:31,761 --> 00:30:33,161
A dish that goes well with rice...

678
00:30:33,261 --> 00:30:34,760
or a dish using rice.

679
00:30:34,761 --> 00:30:36,477
(A dish that goes well with rice or a dish using rice?)

680
00:30:36,501 --> 00:30:37,700
Something healthy...

681
00:30:37,701 --> 00:30:39,901
- and soft.
- Yes, for sure.

682
00:30:41,441 --> 00:30:43,671
I came up with this. Since we're in Gangwon Province,

683
00:30:44,671 --> 00:30:47,411
we can make rice dough balls and perilla seed cream.

684
00:30:48,081 --> 00:30:49,787
- Perilla seed cream.
- Yes, perilla seed cream.

685
00:30:49,811 --> 00:30:51,551
It's super delicious. It's totally...

686
00:30:51,851 --> 00:30:53,480
- Rice dough balls...
- The seniors...

687
00:30:53,481 --> 00:30:55,041
- will like it too.
- Yes. A warm dish.

688
00:30:55,121 --> 00:30:56,927
Perilla seed rice dough balls with thick soup.

689
00:30:56,951 --> 00:30:58,461
I'm from Gangwon Province.

690
00:30:58,721 --> 00:31:00,520
People in Gangwon Province...

691
00:31:00,521 --> 00:31:02,761
enjoy eating perilla seed potato ball soup.

692
00:31:03,131 --> 00:31:04,960
(Potato ball: Local food of Gangwon Province)

693
00:31:04,961 --> 00:31:06,481
(Ground potatoes are made into balls.)

694
00:31:06,601 --> 00:31:08,801
I'll use the Cheorwon rice to make rice dough balls...

695
00:31:09,871 --> 00:31:12,311
and make tteokbokki with them, using not just perilla seed...

696
00:31:12,401 --> 00:31:14,301
but cream too.

697
00:31:14,671 --> 00:31:16,310
It will taste exotic...

698
00:31:16,311 --> 00:31:17,840
and local at the same time.

699
00:31:17,841 --> 00:31:20,280
You should be able to taste various flavours.

700
00:31:20,281 --> 00:31:21,311
That's what I decided on.

701
00:31:21,981 --> 00:31:24,681
(But...)

702
00:31:25,351 --> 00:31:27,121
How do we make rice cake?

703
00:31:28,391 --> 00:31:30,691
(How on earth will he make rice dough balls?)

704
00:31:30,991 --> 00:31:32,591
There was no machine to make rice cakes.

705
00:31:32,821 --> 00:31:34,790
I didn't have a mallet...

706
00:31:34,791 --> 00:31:35,831
nor a mortar.

707
00:31:37,131 --> 00:31:39,031
How will we make rice cakes out of plain rice?

708
00:31:40,761 --> 00:31:42,330
I'm having a rush of dopamine.

709
00:31:42,331 --> 00:31:43,331
This is fun.

710
00:31:47,041 --> 00:31:49,471
Should I chop down a tree and make a mallet?

711
00:31:51,141 --> 00:31:52,381
A tree...

712
00:31:52,841 --> 00:31:53,841
Someone else's tree.

713
00:31:54,381 --> 00:31:56,351
And a thought flashed in my mind.

714
00:31:58,781 --> 00:32:00,421
I saw something just now.

715
00:32:02,791 --> 00:32:05,861
(Looking around)

716
00:32:06,261 --> 00:32:08,091
Here it is.

717
00:32:10,031 --> 00:32:12,861
(What caught Kwan Hoon's eyes...)

718
00:32:18,501 --> 00:32:20,001
Okay.

719
00:32:20,771 --> 00:32:23,710
There was a rolling pin, which was also made of trees.

720
00:32:23,711 --> 00:32:26,310
You're supposed to use it to roll dough, actually.

721
00:32:26,311 --> 00:32:28,510
I choose you.

722
00:32:28,511 --> 00:32:29,550
(It will be his mallet for the day.)

723
00:32:29,551 --> 00:32:31,850
I was only thinking about how I could make rice cakes.

724
00:32:31,851 --> 00:32:34,221
I just went "whatever" and took it with me.

725
00:32:34,581 --> 00:32:35,591
Nice.

726
00:32:36,451 --> 00:32:37,521
Let's go now.

727
00:32:37,991 --> 00:32:39,060
(Freshly-steamed Cheorwon Odae Rice)

728
00:32:39,061 --> 00:32:40,301
There are so many things to do.

729
00:32:41,461 --> 00:32:43,941
For the first time in my life, I used a blender to blend rice.

730
00:32:51,631 --> 00:32:53,870
I blended it until 30 percent of the rice maintained its form.

731
00:32:53,871 --> 00:32:54,871
That's because...

732
00:32:54,872 --> 00:32:57,441
rice dough ball feels very chewy...

733
00:32:58,211 --> 00:33:00,441
when it still has the bit of texture to it.

734
00:33:01,241 --> 00:33:03,510
(The 1st round of chewy rice dough is completed.)

735
00:33:03,511 --> 00:33:05,450
And to add the stickiness,

736
00:33:05,451 --> 00:33:07,950
I added potato starch, the amount being 20 percent...

737
00:33:07,951 --> 00:33:09,190
of the total amount of rice.

738
00:33:09,191 --> 00:33:11,820
I prepared the dough as if I was making potato balls.

739
00:33:11,821 --> 00:33:15,061
(20 percent potato starch, 80 percent rice)

740
00:33:19,431 --> 00:33:21,531
(100 percent handmade rice dough balls)

741
00:33:22,601 --> 00:33:25,741
(The dough starts getting stickier.)

742
00:33:26,471 --> 00:33:29,111
Okay, we can do it.

743
00:33:30,211 --> 00:33:33,681
(Very serious)

744
00:33:36,081 --> 00:33:37,881
I can do it.

745
00:33:44,651 --> 00:33:45,791
Good.

746
00:33:46,121 --> 00:33:47,321
The bat is a bit small.

747
00:33:51,431 --> 00:33:53,631
If air pockets form inside the rice cake...

748
00:33:54,401 --> 00:33:56,201
and you boil it or steam it,

749
00:33:56,531 --> 00:33:59,440
the inside becomes hollow like bread.

750
00:33:59,441 --> 00:34:01,240
When that happens,

751
00:34:01,241 --> 00:34:03,241
the chewy texture disappears.

752
00:34:06,781 --> 00:34:08,311
You're making more work for yourself.

753
00:34:10,611 --> 00:34:13,181
You should either beat it good with a mallet...

754
00:34:13,681 --> 00:34:15,220
or press it down using a machine to push out the air.

755
00:34:15,221 --> 00:34:16,391
It's called compressibility.

756
00:34:17,321 --> 00:34:20,060
(Compressing)

757
00:34:20,061 --> 00:34:21,061
Darn it.

758
00:34:21,591 --> 00:34:22,711
I might break the worktable.

759
00:34:24,091 --> 00:34:27,230
It was an impossible situation, but I tried to make rice cake.

760
00:34:27,231 --> 00:34:28,971
It's insane, but as I did it...

761
00:34:29,631 --> 00:34:30,671
Look at how sticky it is.

762
00:34:31,231 --> 00:34:32,951
The more I hit, the more glutinous it gets.

763
00:34:33,441 --> 00:34:36,271
When I felt that it was turning into rice cake in a way,

764
00:34:37,171 --> 00:34:38,911
I felt a burst of dopamine and thrill.

765
00:34:39,511 --> 00:34:41,611
I enjoyed the sense of accomplishment.

766
00:34:42,411 --> 00:34:44,080
I watched him from the side,

767
00:34:44,081 --> 00:34:45,111
and he looked like...

768
00:34:45,521 --> 00:34:49,021
a woman beating her laundry with a laundry bat by the stream.

769
00:34:50,621 --> 00:34:52,591
(Oiling the tray)

770
00:34:53,091 --> 00:34:55,890
(Now that he's made the rice cake dough, )

771
00:34:55,891 --> 00:34:58,631
(it's time to shape it.)

772
00:35:00,931 --> 00:35:04,371
(Rolling it into balls)

773
00:35:06,001 --> 00:35:08,971
(Made rice dough ball)

774
00:35:11,141 --> 00:35:14,180
Little did I think I'd make rice-based tteok on the spot.

775
00:35:14,181 --> 00:35:15,311
I know, right?

776
00:35:16,681 --> 00:35:17,951
Tteokbokki exists everywhere.

777
00:35:19,281 --> 00:35:22,091
The texture feels a lot like glutinous mochi.

778
00:35:23,251 --> 00:35:24,451
The colour is spot on.

779
00:35:24,591 --> 00:35:26,121
It's white as snow.

780
00:35:27,261 --> 00:35:29,961
(Everything About Tteokbokki)

781
00:35:32,101 --> 00:35:33,731
My flattery skills are going to improve.

782
00:35:34,761 --> 00:35:38,131
But I have no one to flatter now.

783
00:35:39,471 --> 00:35:41,741
(CEO of a 270 million dollar annual revenue company)

784
00:35:44,271 --> 00:35:47,241
(Rolled out about 300 rice dough balls)

785
00:35:50,681 --> 00:35:51,751
It's hot.

786
00:35:57,191 --> 00:35:58,790
Done. The balls are floating.

787
00:35:58,791 --> 00:36:00,951
(Scooping out the floating rice balls with a strainer)

788
00:36:01,421 --> 00:36:03,891
(Rinsing them in cold water)

789
00:36:05,461 --> 00:36:07,460
(Rice dough balls are done!)

790
00:36:07,461 --> 00:36:08,501
Okay.

791
00:36:09,871 --> 00:36:13,801
As for the sauce, I'm cooking onions...

792
00:36:15,371 --> 00:36:17,871
and garlic in butter for the aroma first.

793
00:36:19,311 --> 00:36:23,111
Into that mix, fresh cream and milk are added in a 2-to-1 ratio.

794
00:36:24,981 --> 00:36:27,751
Button mushrooms are thrown into the pot for that mushroom aroma.

795
00:36:28,581 --> 00:36:29,691
There's one key ingredient.

796
00:36:30,091 --> 00:36:33,590
Two pieces of peperoncino.

797
00:36:33,591 --> 00:36:36,891
I wanted the customers to feel, "Wait! A clean aftertaste?"

798
00:36:37,361 --> 00:36:39,831
That was why I only added two pieces of peperoncino.

799
00:36:44,531 --> 00:36:47,100
Lastly, I mixed in a generous amount of perilla seed powder.

800
00:36:47,101 --> 00:36:48,577
(Adding in the rice dough balls and perilla seed powder last)

801
00:36:48,601 --> 00:36:50,810
It's so that the customers can taste...

802
00:36:50,811 --> 00:36:52,981
the aroma of Gangwon Province's perilla seeds.

803
00:36:54,581 --> 00:36:57,181
(Perilla seed cream rice dough ball)

804
00:36:57,981 --> 00:36:59,021
All set.

805
00:37:03,191 --> 00:37:06,861
It is currently 12:24pm.

806
00:37:07,891 --> 00:37:10,491
